{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":1144773,"reputation":161,"user_id":35847,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/yrVwdZs0.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Lakshay Dulani","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35847/lakshay-dulani"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1590773537,"post_id":39955,"comment_id":83545,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Same with us except my daughter used to sleep in the bassinet for the first month. After that she did the same as ur daughter. Now she is 15 months old but still sleeps on her mother feed latched. Doctor says this habit is not good but we couldn&#39;t make her sleep otherwise. Also don&#39;t worry about sleeping 11 hours."},{"owner":{"account_id":6944450,"reputation":155,"user_id":38851,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c1122c6a032c2f8fadce8ad5cb4219ff?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"David","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38851/david"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1590848303,"post_id":39955,"comment_id":83563,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for your helpful comments everyone! We are meeting with a lactation consultant this week to check some things"},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1590864799,"post_id":39955,"comment_id":83566,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The best “lactation consultant” we had had 4 kids of her own and countless babies as she was a midwife just about to retire... a trackrecord that is hard to beat. The first one was just out of studies and had no kids..."},{"owner":{"account_id":6944450,"reputation":155,"user_id":38851,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c1122c6a032c2f8fadce8ad5cb4219ff?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"David","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38851/david"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1590929351,"post_id":39955,"comment_id":83572,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks @SolarMike . We met with one already who wasn’t that helpful. This one has a lot of positive reviews and is recommended by our pediatricians office, so hopefully she’ll be better."},{"owner":{"account_id":460022,"reputation":13472,"user_id":4348,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":85,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/180e84ff9ac31adb32548846e6cc9702?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"SomeShinyObject","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4348/someshinyobject"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1591593799,"post_id":40024,"comment_id":83685,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Can you please put all of the new details from your comments into the original post and also add a clear question to go along with it?"},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":460022,"reputation":13472,"user_id":4348,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":85,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/180e84ff9ac31adb32548846e6cc9702?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"SomeShinyObject","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4348/someshinyobject"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1592228267,"post_id":40024,"comment_id":83723,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi Chef - I have edited your comments into the post, but I still am not sure what your core question is. If you can articulate that, as @SomeShinyObject says, we can look at reopening."},{"owner":{"account_id":1530716,"reputation":3476,"user_id":36371,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/48248a8939c6db9b3d90f02b72354198?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"stan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36371/stan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1592420121,"post_id":40024,"comment_id":83776,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Being awake in the night is <i>boring</i>. It&#39;s all dark, there are no toys, it&#39;s totally not interesting. Being awake during the day is <i>fun!</i> it&#39;s light and time to play. So what if he&#39;s up in the night? If he&#39;s happy, leave him be. As long as he&#39;s averaging enough sleep time in general. A child his age doesn&#39;t need to be fed overnight. Remember also he may not necessarily be sleeping for the entire naptime, unless you&#39;re watching him every moment you won&#39;t know. My 2yo daughter has a dedicated 2 hour window for naps (1pm to 3pm) and she doesn&#39;t always sleep the entire time."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":14,"creation_date":1593097523,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83877,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Just a note for our American users - it sounds like Matthias is using the word &quot;Kindergarten&quot; in the European sense, meaning &quot;daycare/preschool&quot; as Americans would use it, not the 5 year old school grade.  :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1593104768,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83880,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;So far she has never been violent in the sense of hitting or scratching other children. So we think this is not a problematic thing.&quot; Depends what you mean by &quot;problematic&quot;, because demanding toys from others and snatching is still bullying."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593115133,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83883,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi! I think most of our other [pacifier] questions should give you good guidance on this, and I&#39;m not sure if there&#39;s a specific question you&#39;re asking that isn&#39;t covered by some of these:"},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593115147,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83884,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/4984/is-it-bad-to-use-a-pacifier?rq=1\" title=\"is it bad to use a pacifier\">parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/4984/&hellip;</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593115191,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83885,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/88/when-should-a-child-stop-using-a-pacifier-and-how-can-i-help-with-this-transiti\" title=\"when should a child stop using a pacifier and how can i help with this transiti\">parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/88/&hellip;</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593118459,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83887,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@deworde I wouldn&#39;t use the first one, that&#39;s for a newborn - totally different situation.  The second question seems more appropriate here as a duplicate."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593119673,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83889,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe The problem here is that it isn&#39;t clear what&#39;s being asked beyond &quot;Is a pacifier bad?&quot;. Answers to the question I linked to link to multiple sources about pacifier use going up to at least 12 months."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593119728,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83890,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@deworde The first question though is specific to a newborn - a 0-3 month old.  This is not a newborn, this is a 9 month old, so the second question is definitely more appropriate.  The first question&#39;s accepted answer talks about nipple confusion, for example - that&#39;s not a risk for a 9 month old ;)"},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593119776,"post_id":40123,"comment_id":83891,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe Read that answer to the end."},{"owner":{"account_id":14294318,"reputation":143,"user_id":39050,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GgeiyghCmGJZVikjGTZCCVSedMhyW-CPYpBCFgh=k-s256","display_name":"Matthias Mohr","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39050/matthias-mohr"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593151907,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83892,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@deworde: Exactly that is why I sometimes tend to stop or actually stop her from doing this. On the other hand we already let the older children handle it themselves, which worked."},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1593185199,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83897,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@deworde a 17 <i>month</i> old child isn&#39;t &quot;bullying&quot; by taking a toy. A 17 <i>year</i> old is. At 17 months, the concepts of &quot;right&quot; and &quot;wrong&quot; are rudimentary, at best."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1593189058,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83901,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Freeman It doesn&#39;t carry the same moral weight, but the act is the same as when they&#39;re 6 or 7, and if you don&#39;t teach them It&#39;s wrong, why would they stop?"},{"owner":{"account_id":2717324,"reputation":409,"user_id":33670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/MnkGM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"FreeMan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33670/freeman"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1593189385,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83902,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@deworde taking someone else&#39;s toy is, indeed wrong, and a child should be taught not to. At under 2 years old, though, it&#39;s not &quot;bullying&quot; because the kid doesn&#39;t understand that it&#39;s wrong, and at that age, you cannot expect the kid to understand. By the time the kid&#39;s 4, 5, 6 (depending on the child), one certainly <i>can</i> expect her to know that, and the punishment for continuing in the wrong behavior grows more severe as the age increases. When you&#39;re 18 (in the US) and you take someone else&#39;s toys, we call it theft and throw you in jail. You don&#39;t do that to a 2yo."},{"owner":{"account_id":1951789,"reputation":237,"user_id":26461,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/J3dKX.png?s=256","display_name":"Nat","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26461/nat"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1593248772,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83909,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Might help to <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/posts/40126/edit\">edit</a> the question to refer to this behavior as &quot;<i>selfish</i>&quot; rather than &quot;<i>dominant</i>&quot;.  Dominant behavior would be more like trying to control the other children, but this sounds like a typical stage of selfishness."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1593338438,"post_id":40126,"comment_id":83916,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Yes, but you still call it stealing at 8, even if you don&#39;t get the police to caution them under the 1968 Theft Act. My point is not that you need to treat it as a moral failing on the part of a 17 month old, but merely that you need to treat the <i>behaviour</i> as &quot;problematic &quot; at any age; <i>which we actually agree on</i>."},{"owner":{"account_id":14410,"reputation":2980,"user_id":25791,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/J38sS.jpg?s=256","display_name":"T.E.D.","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25791/t-e-d"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1594151841,"post_id":40158,"comment_id":83995,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Man....I had one of those and nothing worked but patience (and even that failed me sometimes). Bless you."},{"owner":{"account_id":509441,"reputation":301,"user_id":14590,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e5c218c8db73eff4f8bb148cab6121f8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Russell McMahon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14590/russell-mcmahon"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1594431140,"post_id":40173,"comment_id":84020,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Just &quot;for fun&quot;: I&#39;m 69 (years, not months). About 5 years ago I had a C4-C5 spinal fusion. Just about every consequence of this was extremely positive. However, it seems my throat must now be somewhat narrower &quot;part of the way down&quot; . The maximum size food portion that will &quot;go down&quot; has been reduced noticeably. While 5 years on I&#39;ve largely learned what my new limits are this is not always the case and I occasionally find myself gagging on food and wholly unable to swallow something that would have gone down with ease previously. The occasional very embarrassing moment has occurred :-) :-(."},{"owner":{"account_id":138391,"reputation":1320,"user_id":1419,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/23bea3cac63585e10858de10f2c6b478?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dotancohen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/1419/dotancohen"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1594563213,"post_id":40173,"comment_id":84028,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It seems that breadsticks might actually be the best thing to teach him to chew properly. They may be a bit painful, but are less dangerous than more solid foods such as apple or pear. I would continue with the breadsticks until he learns."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1594670975,"post_id":40206,"comment_id":84059,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What is your typical nighttime routine (bath, reading, feeding, etc)? This may help answer your question."},{"owner":{"account_id":14683104,"reputation":1044,"user_id":38387,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FjDrN.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephanie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38387/stephanie"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1594681218,"post_id":40206,"comment_id":84062,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Can you give more context on what the goal is and what you’re currently doing that’s not working? What do you mean by he begins to move, do you mean toss and turn? Thanks!"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1595004028,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":84100,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I don&#39;t have time to research this for a full answer, but there&#39;s a <i>ton</i> of research on laughter in babies.  <a href=\"https://ideas.ted.com/meet-a-scientist-with-a-most-delightful-job-he-studies-baby-laughter/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">This TedTalk</a> might be a good place to start looking, or some of the references in <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4424150/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this paper on parental affect</a> might also be good places to look.  There&#39;s no question that it&#39;s good for development to laugh - emotional, if not intellectual, development for sure.  Someone may just need to find the studies for you."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1595004093,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":84101,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"One note though - <i>tickling</i> induced laughter is a bit different from other things, from what I&#39;ve seen.  Tickling laughter is a reflex, and can actually accompany feelings of pain or unhappiness but still include laughter - not always, or not even often for most kids, but do be aware it&#39;s different from laughter brought on by an emotional state or a reaction to something they perceive visually or orally."},{"owner":{"account_id":18868,"reputation":2931,"user_id":7479,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6c2dd6a39d3f38f1bb47a8c1fe8325e2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"sleske","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7479/sleske"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1595491440,"post_id":40051,"comment_id":84117,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"WHAT??? It seems your doctor is putting your child in danger! Please read this: &quot;Promethazine [the active substance in Phenargan] may cause breathing to slow or stop, and may cause death in children.&quot; (source: <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/druginfo/meds/a682284.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">MedlinePlus Drug Info</a>). Please do not give Phenargan without at least consulting another medical professional!"},{"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1595537903,"post_id":40051,"comment_id":84124,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you tried putting her in a stroller or carrying her when she is like this, as an alternative to car-rides? I can symphatize, super-cranky baby is no fun but these things happen and she will grow over it."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1595732550,"post_id":40257,"comment_id":84152,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"How much &quot;tummy time&quot; is the baby getting?"},{"owner":{"account_id":356157,"reputation":249,"user_id":39215,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ee7dba0113ba15076c79d0e9043d7718?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Thomas","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39215/thomas"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1595755157,"post_id":40257,"comment_id":84153,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A few times a day, she just learned to hold her head up while on the tummy. In the past few weeks she started to push a lot on her legs to move forward so there is definitely some rubbing the hair from playing too"},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1595827686,"post_id":40257,"comment_id":84157,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Side note: Carrying her “too much” isn’t an issue, we humans are built to be carried around by others while small."},{"owner":{"account_id":540670,"reputation":177,"user_id":28794,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/12cb1f05ca5db34fc95b1171889de33e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Korinna","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28794/korinna"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1595832406,"post_id":40246,"comment_id":84158,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I had the same problem with my son. I used a travel bed with a good matress for several month. Worked well. Example for the travel bed I used (am from Germany, they are very common here): <a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/B07YRH21MJ\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">amazon.com/Comfortable-Playard-Sturdy-Play-Mattress/dp/&hellip;</a>  Edit: found out it&#39;s called a play yard in USA. We use them as travel bed. Just replace the matress with a good one."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1596529899,"post_id":40302,"comment_id":84265,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi Emily, I think with a question of child safety, any responsible answer would simply direct you to either the <a href=\"https://www.enfamil.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Enfamil website</a> or to the guidelines on the product. For the same reason the site doesn&#39;t allow medical advice: if someone told you X would be fine and your child got sick (or if someone with a subtly different situation misapplied that advice), that would be appalling."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1596564702,"post_id":40302,"comment_id":84270,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Emily, Can you please edit your post to include how old your baby is, what the &quot;best by&quot; and &quot;use by&quot; dates are on your can, and whether the baby is formula fed, or if you only supplement breastfeeding? Was the package in the sun or the shade? Thanks."},{"owner":{"account_id":460022,"reputation":13472,"user_id":4348,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":85,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/180e84ff9ac31adb32548846e6cc9702?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"SomeShinyObject","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4348/someshinyobject"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1596607238,"post_id":40302,"comment_id":84273,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Comments are not for extended discussion; this conversation has been <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/111434/discussion-on-question-by-emily-smith-enfamil-powder-left-outside-in-heat\">moved to chat</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1597007660,"post_id":40318,"comment_id":84292,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"With your wife having disabilities herself, can you afford a mostly full time caregiver? You have a lot of medical and some psychological issues that would make having a baby extra stressful. If you&#39;re quite serious about this, you and your wife should be talking this over with a family therapist who can give better advice, and any family members who you would depend on for help if not independently well-to-do. Almost anyone can do it with enough help. (Babies pretty much guarantee sleep deprivation for at least a couple of months.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1361338,"reputation":133,"user_id":39291,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bxBzT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jonathan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39291/jonathan"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1597055131,"post_id":40318,"comment_id":84298,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"That is a wonderful idea.  I currently cannot afford it, but I do have a video game business that I am trying to get off the ground, making and selling games.  I also work a computer job 9-5 for my main income, but we are currently struggling to make ends meet.  If I can get my business off the ground though, there is some hope there.  I wonder if someone might be willing to be a live-in nanny to handle the baby at night and when we are unable for a free place to stay, and a small paycheck, and where a good place to look would be?"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1597067524,"post_id":40318,"comment_id":84299,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Those people are called <i>au pairs</i>.  ;)  Yes, they live with the family, and yes, it&#39;s for room, board, small salary, but the purported reason people become <i>au pairs</i> is to visit another country (they are usually foreigners), and they need time off to do things with other <i>au pairs</i>. They live with the family, and should be treated as valuable family members (which can be stressful in its own way), not paid help. I think this is a valuable possibility to explore. I wish you the best of luck. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1361338,"reputation":133,"user_id":39291,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bxBzT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jonathan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39291/jonathan"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1597090930,"post_id":40318,"comment_id":84303,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you, that may be a real possibility, this gives me some real hope.  I will have to look more into that, it might be a viable option!"},{"owner":{"account_id":1361338,"reputation":133,"user_id":39291,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bxBzT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jonathan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39291/jonathan"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1597110804,"post_id":40318,"comment_id":84306,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"After doing research, apparently au pairs cost about 30K a year.  That is currently outside our reach unless I can get a serious raise or get my business to take off.  This is a more reachable goal than 50k a year for a nanny though.  I really appreciate the feedback, thank you!  If you or anyone else has more ideas, I am listening!"},{"owner":{"account_id":1361338,"reputation":133,"user_id":39291,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bxBzT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jonathan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39291/jonathan"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1597111714,"post_id":40318,"comment_id":84307,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I will have to see on the family front.  Unfortunately my dad lives far away, and my mom is no longer in the picture.  Other relatives are older and probably unable to help us through the nights.  It might not hurt to check though.  I have extreme issues when facing sleep deprivation - I would seriously rather have my hands cut off, or endure other severe torture than that.  I want a kid though, and my wife does too.  I don&#39;t think I can deal with the sleep deprivation without going insane though, and loosing my ability to work.  I don&#39;t know what to do, and am open to advice."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1598282707,"post_id":40394,"comment_id":84478,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What kind of surface is the child crawling on?  Hardwood, concrete, carpet, rug, etc.?  What clothes are the baby wearing - diaper? shorts? pants?  And, is the skin just rough, or is it bleeding?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1796213,"reputation":3431,"user_id":21986,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/991a4a6ddb04ff0a0d0ca93849f9b0ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"learner101","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21986/learner101"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1598293398,"post_id":40394,"comment_id":84479,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe Mainly the bed and carpeted floor. He wears pants more often these days, since i noticed the knees. And there&#39;s no bleeding, its just rough skin"},{"owner":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1598488835,"post_id":40400,"comment_id":84488,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Not a full answer, but could you put him in time out by sticking him in a play yard where he can&#39;t get his hands on anything dangerous whenever he starts throwing / biting / hammering?  That might not be a bad way of providing consistent punishment / natural consequences for being violent."},{"owner":{"account_id":301392,"reputation":133,"user_id":20336,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/acd291d2a2f923745ed916b35356f1eb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"PhD","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/20336/phd"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1598505464,"post_id":40400,"comment_id":84492,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He can mostly climb over everything. But we have a baby gate in a place where he can see us and we do put him in that area. Sorta starts having fun after a while 🤷‍♂️"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1598887127,"post_id":40419,"comment_id":84528,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi Michu, welcome to the site.  I&#39;ve edited your question to make it less of an &quot;opinion&quot; question and more factually answerable.  Please edit further if I misunderstood your question.  Thanks, and welcome!"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1599915667,"post_id":40507,"comment_id":84838,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Are you worried there&#39;s something wrong, that she&#39;s behind, or tired of her not walking on her own? In other words, <i>why</i> do you want her to stand/want to encourage her to stand alone? Within ranges, kids normally develop at the pace that is &quot;comfortable&quot; for them physically. She drops down because she&#39;s not comfortable with it yet. They walk soon enough."},{"owner":{"account_id":13967236,"reputation":2133,"user_id":38277,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe2c499031dc8c8e0e4c8f5956d9e967?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"A.bakker","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38277/a-bakker"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1600024057,"post_id":40509,"comment_id":84839,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What country do you live in? this would help giving better advice on how to deal with authorities. Also what methods of discipline have you tried?"},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1600098039,"post_id":40509,"comment_id":84841,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi Misty, as A.bakker asked, we need some details - what jurisdiction are you in, why do the police say they won&#39;t help, do you have a reference from the psych ward who could help progress treatment/custody etc."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1600098843,"post_id":40509,"comment_id":84842,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Please provide helpful details. If he was in a psych ward, he&#39;s in the psychiatric  system. How are they failing you/your son? Did you call the police when he broke his brother&#39;s arm?  In the Emergency Room, we call Crisis Intervention when a sibling is injured. Did the school press charges for the attacks/have Children&amp;Youth/Crisis Intervention been called? What was the outcome? (Btw, <i>treating</i> this <i>is</i> beyond the scope of of the average doctor. He needs a psychiatrist to help treat him, which happens in a psych facility.) And a <i>criminal</i> type Conduct Disorder <i>is</i> a police matter."},{"owner":{"account_id":977054,"reputation":101,"user_id":39279,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/787b0a0e1ac4e24e933b398f030de766?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"stuart10","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39279/stuart10"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1600703026,"post_id":40522,"comment_id":84954,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s probably worth thinking about why her sleep is not good when on her back and then seeing if you can do anything about that. From your description, it sounds like she might have silent reflux."},{"owner":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1600782722,"post_id":40548,"comment_id":84959,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you asked your pediatrician about this issue specifically?  Did they have any recommendations?  (I see they said something about sleep training but it isn&#39;t clear if that was in reference to this issue or was part of another conversation.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":6944450,"reputation":155,"user_id":38851,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c1122c6a032c2f8fadce8ad5cb4219ff?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"David","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38851/david"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1600782892,"post_id":40548,"comment_id":84960,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It was in reference to this issue.  She said &quot;do whatever it takes&quot;.  She said that based on what we told her, it doesn&#39;t seem like our daughter knows how to self-soothe, so sleep-training now would just result in her crying herself to exhaustion.  I&#39;m curious if others who sleep trained had indications that their kid knew how to self-soothe.  What were those indications?  At night, our daughter is able to connect sleep cycles, sucks her thumb, and sleeps in stretches of 3-5 hours.  Naps are around 30-40 mins."},{"owner":{"account_id":6944450,"reputation":155,"user_id":38851,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c1122c6a032c2f8fadce8ad5cb4219ff?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"David","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38851/david"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1600782960,"post_id":40548,"comment_id":84961,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s the initial getting to sleep that is a dang constant battle.  She doesn&#39;t know how to put herself to sleep.  Sometimes it&#39;s a 45-60 minute battle of putting her to sleep for a 30 minute nap."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1600865243,"post_id":40548,"comment_id":84970,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"David - a good thing to remember is that most parents go through this. It is exhausting, but normal."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601217061,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85043,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You all have my sympathy. Breast engorgement hurts, and breastfeeding is hard on moms of newborns under normal circumstances, let alone these added stressors However, you need to discuss this with your baby&#39;s doctor. If your baby&#39;s serum bilirubin was high enough to place an NG tube, it&#39;s important to their health. For engorgement, a good breast pump is a life-saver. (Fyi: breast and bottle fed my first for months.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1492517,"reputation":111,"user_id":39643,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FoRYg.png?s=256","display_name":"Bondolin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39643/bondolin"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601217378,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85045,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you. It&#39;s not an officially mandated feeding tube, still getting acquainted with the correct terminology. Really just an oral tube attached to a syringe pumping Similac, suggested by the LC but not required. We&#39;re just using it to entice the little guy into a good latch. We&#39;ve had some success with him finishing the syringe and pulling the tube, and he staying latched well, but last night was super rough. Might be he&#39;s just too tired and impatient, so letting him sleep a little longer, feeding after 5 hours rather than the normal 3. That is giving mommy a break too."},{"owner":{"account_id":1492517,"reputation":111,"user_id":39643,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FoRYg.png?s=256","display_name":"Bondolin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39643/bondolin"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601217421,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85046,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Also, working on getting a breast pump through covering insurance."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601218163,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85047,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;ve not practiced pediatric medicine in a long time; never heard of this. It&#39;s clear that this is a really difficult time. I can&#39;t say much more; that would be me trying to assess a situation that I have no business in. As far as a breast pump goes, buy a cheap one while waiting on your insurance carrier for the better version. It sounds like your baby is having a hard time, too. You might want to read about letting them sleep longer; it may be compounding the situation (wake up hungrier, frustrated waiting for let-down, etc.) Again, call your baby&#39;s doc. This is what they&#39;re there for."},{"owner":{"account_id":1492517,"reputation":111,"user_id":39643,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FoRYg.png?s=256","display_name":"Bondolin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39643/bondolin"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601237208,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85049,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We have a second hand pump we are using for preventing the engorging for now, until we get our own."},{"owner":{"account_id":1492517,"reputation":111,"user_id":39643,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FoRYg.png?s=256","display_name":"Bondolin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39643/bondolin"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601237222,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85050,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Pediatrician visiting now."},{"owner":{"account_id":7813880,"reputation":7904,"user_id":30366,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f996f3ea5fc3e9daa68d0f98db62b6be?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Meg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30366/meg"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601392588,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85062,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think the tube in this case is maybe an SNS - &quot;supplemental nursing system&quot;?  And not an NG tube?  Is this correct?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1492517,"reputation":111,"user_id":39643,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FoRYg.png?s=256","display_name":"Bondolin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39643/bondolin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601393881,"post_id":40575,"comment_id":85063,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It was an enteral/oral feeding tube screwed into a syringe. Just google monoject syringe should do it."},{"owner":{"account_id":7411060,"reputation":1882,"user_id":21004,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e1cd8b1a9ef4cee3389a3d36241e618a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"MakorDal","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21004/makordal"},"edited":false,"score":5,"creation_date":1601463845,"post_id":40584,"comment_id":85068,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Good luck with your problem. How long do you let her cry ?"},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601478637,"post_id":39519,"comment_id":85071,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@AlanMcLean Do you have any updates on what worked best? Thank you!"},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1601489391,"post_id":40584,"comment_id":85073,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"For other relevant answers, search on this site for <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/search?q=ferber\">“Ferber”</a>. For example: <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/q/39575\">parenting.stackexchange.com/q/39575</a> , <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/q/40158\">parenting.stackexchange.com/q/40158</a> , <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/q/39519\">parenting.stackexchange.com/q/39519</a> , <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/q/40548\">parenting.stackexchange.com/q/40548</a> ,"},{"owner":{"account_id":511031,"reputation":101,"user_id":39657,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/36adaa21b66ffa195e6f5d47d3453103?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Beni Bogosel","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39657/beni-bogosel"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1601497923,"post_id":40584,"comment_id":85074,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We also had a similar issue with our son. We resisted up to when he was 15 months. Then he became too heavy and we needed to train him to sleep on his own. The first few days he cried when we put him in his bed, but we were right near him to comfort him. After a week he became used to the idea and was quite happy to stay quietly in his bed until he fell asleep. The really hard part is not to give up when you hear the baby cry. I was very weak, but my wife was stronger... Now we can&#39;t believe that he always goes to bed by himself. Also giving him a teddy bear to hold may help."},{"owner":{"account_id":2765,"reputation":101,"user_id":9156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/aac3ed50382d1bf4dcc7b91b4bb94ef4?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ed Griebel","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9156/ed-griebel"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601642689,"post_id":40584,"comment_id":85084,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I have no solutions better than the ones proposed, just support. It&#39;s so hard to listen to your child cry when you know that it is in your power to stop it and give them comfort. But you can do it, and they can do it too!! Great advice someone once gave us was, &quot;Don&#39;t worry, they won&#39;t be doing _______ when they&#39;re 18. This is temporary.&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601864324,"post_id":40611,"comment_id":85108,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Is your daughter in a bed or a crib?"},{"owner":{"account_id":3170300,"reputation":131,"user_id":39682,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fb7fe536b9d0799117c012b5e4f60510?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Mikhail","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39682/mikhail"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1601879388,"post_id":40611,"comment_id":85109,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"She always slept in a bed"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1601922353,"post_id":40615,"comment_id":85121,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"How are his sleeping habits?  At that age I usually could find sleep-related issues causing this sort of thing - has anything changed?  Sleeping more, sleeping less, harder time going to sleep, easier time going to sleep?  In particular, are you finding he falls asleep in the car on the way home, assuming you drive (and if so, is that new)?"},{"owner":{"account_id":3007589,"reputation":8375,"user_id":13196,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/TGGMQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Erik","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13196/erik"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1601976753,"post_id":40617,"comment_id":85123,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Requesting medical advice is off-topic here. You&#39;ll have to contact a doctor."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3007589,"reputation":8375,"user_id":13196,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/TGGMQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Erik","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13196/erik"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1601977843,"post_id":40617,"comment_id":85124,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Erik: homeopathic remedies aren&#39;t medicine, though. There&#39;s a simple answer which is a resounding no, this is of no use for anything."},{"owner":{"account_id":3007589,"reputation":8375,"user_id":13196,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/TGGMQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Erik","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13196/erik"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1601977903,"post_id":40617,"comment_id":85125,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@dxh if that&#39;s the stance of this site, I&#39;d totally agree with you that homeopathy doesn&#39;t do anything, but I wasn&#39;t sure."},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":3007589,"reputation":8375,"user_id":13196,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/TGGMQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Erik","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13196/erik"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1602059450,"post_id":40617,"comment_id":85127,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Erik This site doesn&#39;t have a &quot;stance&quot; on any issues except its scope. However like the rest of Stack Exchange it does value evidence-based answers, and the evidence on homoeopathy is very clear."},{"owner":{"account_id":13967236,"reputation":2133,"user_id":38277,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe2c499031dc8c8e0e4c8f5956d9e967?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"A.bakker","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38277/a-bakker"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1604142570,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85385,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"most parents just pick the 3th or the first (o2 28/29 when counting February).. not that big a deal :| unless you got insane parents who say stuff like &quot;My child just turned 1503 days old!&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1604147205,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85387,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"If you want to be precise then you should use seconds...."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1604152827,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85389,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Solar: the question then just becomes how many seconds is a month?"},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1604152954,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85390,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@dxh no, the question becomes how many seconds have elapsed since the birth - easy with a timer..."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1604173557,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85395,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There are 12 months in a year. It doesn&#39;t really matter if they have 28, 29, 30, or 31 days, does it? We say a month. My daily meds are prescribed a month at a time; the prescription states &quot;30 tablets&quot;, which makes me short by a day every other month or so. I don&#39;t really care. In medicine, as in life, a month is roughly 30 days. If a doctor tells a patient on February 1st that they have 3 months to live on, the patient usually doesn&#39;t ask, is that 89, 90, 91, or 92 days? Is that counting today?"},{"owner":{"account_id":2638908,"reputation":101,"user_id":7960,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/eJ0IS.png?s=256","display_name":"NotThatGuy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7960/notthatguy"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1604175944,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85396,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"For what purpose do you need to know how many months they&#39;re old? If it&#39;s just for inconsequential monthly celebrations, then when exactly you celebrate would also be inconsequential. If your doctor is asking, then they most likely don&#39;t expect a 100% perfect answer, or they would ask for the date of birth instead. If it&#39;s for another reason, it&#39;s probably going to depend on the context."},{"owner":{"account_id":1331471,"reputation":1161,"user_id":17160,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2bcb98da58c2be3ee310cd29164c7466?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"zugzwang","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17160/zugzwang"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1604355237,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85417,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This isn&#39;t a parenting question."},{"owner":{"account_id":2515799,"reputation":2235,"user_id":4510,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/eAmJW.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Laurent S.","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4510/laurent-s"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1604414794,"post_id":40721,"comment_id":85423,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It depends if that 31st October was on a leap year or not..."},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605198254,"post_id":40753,"comment_id":85453,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Presumably you want your baby on your wife&#39;s insurance asap to make sure any medical bills will be covered by the insurance. In addition to what you asked for in your question, you could also check with the insurance company what their policy is around newborns and within what time frame you should register your child to get coverage from the date of birth if they offer that option."},{"owner":{"account_id":2222583,"reputation":9991,"user_id":6061,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":59,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/tNrYV.png?s=256","display_name":"Dariusz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6061/dariusz"},"edited":false,"score":35,"creation_date":1605530219,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85495,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Of course he looks excited, there&#39;s moving colors on that previously black rectangle! THAT&#39;S FREAKING AMAZING for him. I&#39;d say it&#39;s actually not good for him, though."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605540710,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85499,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Hi!  I&#39;ve edited your question so it&#39;s topical (&quot;which videos&quot; is off topic) and so it fits the site, and remove the explicit Youtube reference since you&#39;re really asking the more general question.  Please feel free to further edit if I missed some of what you were thinking of.  Also, please see the many questions we have on the <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/tagged/screen-time\">screen-time</a> tag, such as <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/23286/should-a-18-month-old-be-allowed-to-watch-any-videos-online?rq=1\" title=\"should a 18 month old be allowed to watch any videos online\">parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/23286/&hellip;</a> for example; the recommendations for screen time are developing over time, but we have a lot of good questions here."},{"owner":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605575429,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85513,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"If your six-month-old genuinely seems interested, go ahead. If not, why force anything?"},{"owner":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605576835,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85515,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RobbieGoodwin I think that most people would consider your comment to be common sense, but the answers below indicate that common sense may not be best, and may need to be adjusted considerably."},{"owner":{"account_id":12267596,"reputation":101,"user_id":37951,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/69ba6f53aec2c74129c8499e3532d3ef?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Issel","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37951/issel"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1605587697,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85517,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Growing up in a caring, stable, loving household does a great deal more for a child than his/her education. Patience and understanding when your kid doesn&#39;t get things right away (which happens). It&#39;s really hard balance between being there and being a helicopter parent, but that&#39;s far more important for a child to be successful."},{"owner":{"account_id":5962695,"reputation":183,"user_id":27841,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cDBWJ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"henning no longer feeds AI","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27841/henning-no-longer-feeds-ai"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605603834,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85522,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s best to keep your child off youtube and other social media until late into his/her childhood. There is still a lot of uncertainty, but <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jpc.13023#:~:text=The%20studies%20identified%20that%20the,social%20media%20and%20self%2Desteem\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">preliminary research</a> suggests that social media isn&#39;t exactly great for your kid&#39;s mental health. Better to not get habituated too soon."},{"owner":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605652784,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85592,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RockPaperLz- Mask it or Casket  No, in fact to the extent Answers here differ from my Comment, they tend to indicate a desire common sense to be over-ruled in favour of whichever agenda."},{"owner":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605653322,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85593,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RobbieGoodwin I&#39;m not understanding your comment. You seem to be disagreeing, but are also simultaneously acknowledging what I wrote (that common sense may need to be changed).  However, our opinions may differ, as your use of the word &quot;agenda&quot; implies you view something as possibly underhanded or manipulative, which I certainly didn&#39;t state, accuse, or imply."},{"owner":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605654066,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85594,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RockPaperLz- Mask it or Casket Precisely, you&#39;re not understanding. I am disagreeing, and not at all acknowledging your suggestion that common sense might need to be changed.  The word &quot;agenda&quot; did specifically mean something underhanded or manipulative and that remains so. What you stated, accused, or implied wouldn&#39;t change anything even if it worked in English.  In my book, common sense is never to be over-ruled.  Why does that not work for you?  Everyone else please, jump in with both feet!"},{"owner":{"account_id":460022,"reputation":13472,"user_id":4348,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":85,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/180e84ff9ac31adb32548846e6cc9702?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"SomeShinyObject","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4348/someshinyobject"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605662202,"post_id":40771,"comment_id":85604,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Comments are not for extended discussion; this conversation has been <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/116349/discussion-on-question-by-user4951-what-can-i-do-to-a-6-month-child-so-she-end-u\">moved to chat</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":7108718,"reputation":101,"user_id":35511,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/7LvDp.png?s=256","display_name":"Kepotx","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35511/kepotx"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605684743,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85616,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A rule of thumb I have seen a 3-6-9-12 threshold. No screen before 3, as young kids need interaction. No personal screen-device before 6. from 3 to 6 years, screen activity should be a familial thing. From 6 to 9, teach them what screen can do. Introduce them to things like photography, computers, or even programming with tools for kids like Scratch. From 9 to 12, start to teach them how internet work and how to protect them from it (remind them that internet is public, that what they post mays stay for a while, and not to beleive everything on it)."},{"owner":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":9083550,"reputation":105,"user_id":39038,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/10157295115100587/picture?type=large","display_name":"Robbie Goodwin","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39038/robbie-goodwin"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605698434,"post_id":40769,"comment_id":85624,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@RobbieGoodwin wrote: <i>&quot; In my book, common sense is never to be over-ruled. Why does that not work for you?&quot;</i>  My reply: Because common sense tells us the world is flat, that invisible viruses don&#39;t exist and certainly can&#39;t possibly kill us, and that the Sun revolves around the Earth."},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6569274,"reputation":1412,"user_id":17787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jTz7e.png?s=256","display_name":"chasly - supports Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17787/chasly-supports-monica"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605822771,"post_id":40771,"comment_id":85664,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@chasly-supportsMonica see this follow up question: <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/40806/to-what-extent-can-parenting-affect-a-childs-future-intelligence-level\" title=\"to what extent can parenting affect a childs future intelligence level\">parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/40806/&hellip;</a>"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":276}